Reject the codes RFC 1951 reserves or leaves unassigned: literal/length
symbols 286 and 287, distance symbols 30 and 31, dynamic tables that
declare them, incomplete codes zlib refuses, and the unused code of an
incomplete table.

RFC 1951 section 3.2.6: "Literal/length values 286-287 will never actually
occur in the compressed data, but participate in the code construction",
and likewise "distance codes 30-31 will never actually occur in the
compressed data". The fixed Huffman code gives all four a code, so a fixed
block can carry any of them. s_length_base, s_length_extra, s_dist_base
and s_dist_extra have no entry for them (their slots hold 0), so tinfl
decoded a reserved length as a match of length 0 and a reserved distance
as distance 0, and carried on: an invalid stream decoded to bytes, where
zlib's inflate refuses it with "invalid literal/length code" or "invalid
distance code". deflate-raw has no checksum to catch it, so the bytes came
back as data (zukomp #94); zlib and gzip caught it only at the trailer,
after delivering output zlib never would.

A dynamic block can declare them too: HLIT is five bits plus 257, so up to
288 codes, and HDIST five bits plus 1, so up to 32. RFC 1951 3.2.7 bounds
HLIT at 286, and zlib's inflate refuses either count past 286 or 30 when it
reads the block header ("too many length or distance symbols"). tinfl did
not check, so a table that declared the reserved codes was accepted, and a
block that then never used one decoded where zlib refuses it. Both counts
are now checked as the header is read, before a byte of the block is
decoded; the per-symbol checks remain for fixed blocks.

An incomplete code is legal in one shape only (3.2.7): a literal/length or
distance table with a single one-bit code, or a distance table with no
codes. zlib's inflate refuses any other incomplete table, and refuses the
unused code if a stream uses it ("invalid distance code"). tinfl accepted a
single code of any length, and left the unused codes as zero lookup
entries, which TINFL_HUFF_DECODE read as symbol 0 with no bits consumed --
distance 1, so a match copied the previous byte where zlib refuses the
stream. Only the fast literal loop checked for a zero entry (states 40 and
54). Incomplete tables are now held to zlib's rule, and their unused
entries decode as a reserved symbol (287 or 31, one bit), which the
per-symbol checks refuse on every path.

The state numbers, 61 to 64, are clear of upstream's and of patch
0004's 60. Written to be upstreamable as-is.

